  "worked_solution": "The center of the expansion is $x=3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-2,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $5,$ i.e., $R\\ge 5.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-2,8).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-2,8).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/assets/38763aa0d87065a01ccdf0d94bca2a4e1d6ef227aeab4aaacb9703246eee6abf.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-3\\notin [-2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $1\\in [-2,8),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $8\\notin [-2,8),$  we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given statements must be true.\"",
